  "notes": "The melodic half of the Cheiron board rather than a knob-turner, and the model is honest about that: the research says he is \"not a drum programmer — his contribution **presumes the Cheiron bed** (2&4 snare, 8th/16th hats) and writes the melodic rhythm to sit in the gaps between kick and snare\". So the three drum deltas authored are readings of that sentence and nothing more — an 8th/16th base weighted towards the 8th, a kick pushed off the strong beats so the gaps exist (`syncopation` 0.44 with a \"1&\" second anchor and a low density), and the board's no-fill culture as a wide `fills` schedule with near-zero hat rolls. His harmonic fingerprint is documented and is where the model earns its place: \"major-key hooks that begin on the relative minor (vi–IV–I) so the chorus resolves upward\", plus \"a late whole-tone modulation for the final chorus\" as his most recognisable structural habit. Hooks are \"built from a 3-note cell repeated with rising harmony, and the title always lands on the strongest beat of the chorus\" — `motifPitchCount` 3–5 with a two-bar phrase and a high `chordToneBias`. His melodies \"avoid the bass register entirely, staying above E3\", so the register floor is MIDI 52 exactly. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it medium-high and says the production-side fields are inferred because he is a writer, not an engineer. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the whole drums block, which is a reading of the stated Cheiron bed, and `bpm.mode` 96.",
